s their craft you know and  another thing that I would do is when I  had my jokes sometimes I would recite  them in other people's styles I would  say to myself like how would Steven  Wright deliver this joke how would  George Carlin deliver this joke because  what that does is those people have very  distinct styles right Steven Wright is  kind of low-key  deadpan dry Carlin is more high-energy  more physical so sometimes you know like  Sam Kinison whoever it is think of  different styles and you know it's  really just an exercise for you to say  like am I getting everything out of this  joke that I can be that that fits my  style you know as you're figuring out  your style because if you try it in  whoever you know fill in the blank if  you tried in Gary Gulman style or  whoever maybe you'll find one little  moment in the joke that wasn't there  before maybe a pause maybe a facial  gesture maybe something physical  maybe you'll be louder because you tend  to be quiet or vice-versa  so yeah I think exploring different  people's voices when you're starting out  is a good mental kind of exploration and  let's take a step back to this to our  writing because it's very romantic as  comedians I'm gonna sit down and write  everyday and I'm and I feel unfortunate  with this comedy group we're in here at  hot breath that we do a write 10 club  every day where we  show up in right for 10 minutes just to  just to have some sort of daily ritual  some sort of daily habit and social  accountability but two hours how how are  you how are you able to commit to this  what mindset or what habits did you set  in place that like I'm doing this no  matter what well first off I would  commend anyone who's doing the 10  minutes because when you establish those  habits that's a positive you know no  matter how much time it is and like you  said accountability and there's a social  aspect of kind of having you know like a  bit of a binding contract of sorts so  all of that is is good stuf